{"authored" [{"question" "q-280507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A toy rocket is launched from the origin with an initial velocity of $(2i+6j)\\,\\text{m/s}$ and a constant acceleration of $(i+5j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Determine the position vector of the rocket after $2$ seconds.\n\n$r=(${{field-1}}\n$i\\,\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$j)\\text{m}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040268/state.json" "file_sha256" "348c86b40af16516a99a035564bfa9fcff6796a53b3c91ee5e5028bbc5812198" "basis" 1425 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040268/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-280507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If a particle moves with constant acceleration $a,$ the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut+\\frac{1}{2}a{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle and $u$ is its initial velocity.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=0i+0j,\\,u=2i+6j,\\,a=i+5j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the rocket after $t=2\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =(0i+0j)+(2i+6j)(2)+\\frac{1}{2}(i+5j)(2)^{2} \\\\  & =(4i+12j)+2(i+5j) \\\\  & =(4i+12j)+(2i+10j) \\\\  & =(\\,6\\,i+22\\,j)\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040268/state.json" "file_sha256" "348c86b40af16516a99a035564bfa9fcff6796a53b3c91ee5e5028bbc5812198" "basis" 1425 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040268/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-280507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040268/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha2